  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   The current optimization method relies on local gradient-based searches, which struggle to escape shallow local minima common in such highly oscillatory problems. I propose using multi-resolution hierarchical optimization combined with coordinatewise cyclic adjustments that maintain problem structure across scales. This approach will:\n   - Use adaptive grid hierarchies from 5 to 400 points, initializing higher resolutions from the best lower-resolution points\n   - Implement separable projection between grids via integral averaging to preserve structure\n   - Introduce cyclic coordinate-updates with directional constraints based on local correlation features\n   - Fuse regularized non-uniform step patterns to enhance phase-matching between coordinates\n   - Key differentiators are:\n     - Explicit maintenance of the circulant correlation structure through penalty terms in grid projection\n     - Optimization of any harmonic feature vector without penalizing its parent projection\n     - Direct support for cyclic optimization patterns implicit in the integration domain\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   We parameterize h(x) \u2248 \u2211_{k} w_k \u03a6_k(x) where \u03a6_k are basis functions. For hierarchical grids (n=400,200,100):\n     - Objective: min(max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx)\n     - Regularization: ||\u2207h||^2 + \u03b2\u2211|oscillation_features| targets local signal structure preservation\n     - Projection between scales: \u0394h = (H_coarse - \u222bh(x)dx_h)'\u00d7H_fine to respect cyclicity \n     - Basis construction: Utilize Fourier series with adjustable harmonics to represent oscillatory features\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   Step 1: Grid initialization\n   - n0 = 400\n   - Grid sizes: [400, 350, 300, ...] decreasing by 50 points\n   - For each grid size n, initialize h = initial_h (from parent or uniform random)\n\n   Step 2: Hierarchical optimization\n   - For each grid size:\n     1. h_grid = optimise_grid(h, target_sum, n, alpha, max_iter)\n     2. If grid size>5, project best solution to next coarser grid\n     3. Use coordinatewise steps along grid, adjusting each point based on:\n        - Local overlap contribution\n        - Neighbor phase alignment\n        - Hopf condition for periodic alignment\n\n   Step 3: Custom optimization algorithm\n   - Use gradient-based method constrained by:\n     1. Box constraints: l \u2264 h_i \u2264 u\n     2. Integral constraint: \u222bh = target\n     3. Regularization: ||\u2207h||\u00b2 + \u03bb|autocorrelation|\n   - Special objective form: C5 = max(|\u0394h cos\u03b8|, |\u0394h sin\u03b8|) for phase angles\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - Default grid sizes: [5, 20, 40, 60, ...] up to 400\n   - Optimization parameters:\n     1. \u03b1-range: [1e-3, 1]\n     2. Gradient steps: 200 max per grid level\n     3. Reparameterization: h_scaled = h/\u221a(sum h\u00b2) to match phase invariance\n   - Suggested variations: \n     - B-spline basis representation\n     - Robust outlier filtering in correlation\n     - Alternative projected gradient methods\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   - Grid processing: Est. iterations = 200*(400/50) \u2248 1600 total evaluations\n   - Projection cost: O(n log n) per grid transition\n   - Time allocation: 90% sensitivity analysis, 10% final polishing\n   - Deadline checks: At each optimization iteration check global budget\n   - Early exit: If max grid (400) optimization completes, revert to best\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Feasibility checks: \n     1. 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n     2. sum(h) = target\n     3. Input validation for any grid size\n   - Finalization:\n     1. Perform C5 evaluation on final solution\n     2. Check finite value (raise exception if undefined)\n     3. Reproject if numerical issues detected\n   - Sample interface:\n        run(seed=42, budget_s=1300)\n        def compute_c5(h): ... (require dx relative scaling)\n        def project_feasible(h, target_sum): ...",
